<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Erie Canal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1825">1825</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Erie Canal is a canal in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> State, opened in 1825, that linked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudson-river">Hudson River</a> at Albany with Lake Erie at Buffalo, about 580 kilometres away. By cutting the cost of moving goods between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-lakes">Great Lakes</a> and the Atlantic, it opened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midwestern-united-states">Midwest</a> to settlement and trade and made New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a> City the leading port of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/erie-canal.jpg" alt="Erie Canal" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Joshua Karn · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sunset_over_the_Erie_Canal_in_North_Tonawanda,_NY..jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
